Pardon my thickheadedness if I fail to see how Dennett's argument
reduces to that. To me, the argument seemed to be that a completely
interactive, to the last realistic detail, complete simulation--or, if
you like, "VR"--would have to reach a combinatorial explosion, that is,
it would have to simulate much too much simultaneous and instantaneous
information, conforming to the subject's every action, than any present
computer could possibly supply. If you had a computer of infinite power,
theoretically you would be able to work it. However, a computer of
infinite power is pretty hard to come by, and it will take quite a few
years before we can come up with one.

I'm truly astounded that you could reduce that passage to "I can't
imagine how realistic VR could work, so it won't, ever." VR can be
"realistic" without being "perfect"; it wasn't even about VR, that was a
side issue. It was more about the kind of sheer power it takes to fully
and interactively simulate a universe. On the grounds that nothing of
infinite power can exist, I dismiss the above theoretical objection.

I would certainly tally the score as 
    Dennet/Starr/Rand 1, Perry/Price 0.

